414
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:50,420
You might worry about choosing the right words or sounding professional.

415
00:17:50,420 --> 00:17:55,580
But the good news is this, workplace English doesn't need to be complicated.

416
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:59,260
Clear and simple English is usually the best English.

417
00:17:59,260 --> 00:18:03,420
Today's conversation will show you how to speak with your manager about work progress,

418
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:06,340
problems, and expectations.

419
00:18:06,340 --> 00:18:08,020
Just listen and relax.

420
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:11,700
Don't try to translate every sentence in your head.

421
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:14,140
Understanding will come naturally with repetition.

422
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:16,740
Alright, let's begin the conversation.

423
00:18:16,740 --> 00:18:19,540
Hi James, do you have a few minutes?

424
00:18:19,540 --> 00:18:22,540
Hi Anna, yes I do, please come in.

425
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:23,540
Thank you.

426
00:18:23,540 --> 00:18:27,040
I didn't want to interrupt but I thought it was a good time to talk.

427
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:30,140
No problem at all, what would you like to discuss?

428
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:34,940
I wanted to talk about my tasks this quarter and make sure I'm on the right track.

429
00:18:34,940 --> 00:18:38,240
That's a very responsible approach, go ahead.

430
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:42,240
First, I wanted to ask about the new project timeline.

431
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:44,740
What specifically would you like to know?

432
00:18:44,740 --> 00:18:48,240
I'm not sure which parts are most urgent right now.

433
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:52,740
That's a good question. The client analysis is the top priority.

434
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:55,740
Okay, so I should focus on that first.

435
00:18:55,740 --> 00:19:00,240
Yes, once that's done you can move on to the presentation slides.

436
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:03,240
That helps a lot. Thank you for clarifying.

437
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:06,240
Of course. Is the workload manageable for you?

438
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:10,740
It's busy but manageable. I just want to plan my time better.

439
00:19:10,740 --> 00:19:14,340
About smart. Planning prevents unnecessary stress.

440
00:19:14,340 --> 00:19:19,340
I agree. I also wanted to ask about expectations for the presentation.

441
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:21,740
Sure. What are you unsure about?

442
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:25,340
Should it be more detailed or more high level?

443
00:19:25,340 --> 00:19:29,840
High level. Focus on key insights, not every detail.

444
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:33,340
Got it. Clear message, fewer details.

445
00:19:33,340 --> 00:19:36,840
Exactly. Imagine the audience is very busy.

446
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:38,240
That makes sense.

447
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:41,740
How confident do you feel about delivering the presentation?

448
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:45,540
Honestly, I feel a bit nervous speaking in English.

449
00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:48,240
Thought's understandable. You're not alone.

450
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:52,440
Sometimes I worry I'll forget words or lose my train of thought.

451
00:19:52,440 --> 00:19:55,840
That happens to everyone, even native speakers.

452
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:57,940
Really? That's good to know.

453
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:02,040
Yes, the key is preparation, not perfect English.

454
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:05,240
I usually practice out loud before meetings.

455
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:07,840
That's an excellent habit. Keep doing that.

456
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:12,320
I also write down a few key phrases to help me stay focused.

457
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:14,220
That's a great strategy.

458
00:20:14,220 --> 00:20:18,020
Thank you. I wanted to ask for some feedback as well.

459
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:20,060
I'm happy to give you feedback.

460
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:24,220
Overall, do you think my performance is meeting expectations?

461
00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:28,200
Yes, absolutely. You're reliable and well-organized.

462
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:29,860
I'm glad to hear that.

463
00:20:29,860 --> 00:20:34,060
One thing you could work on is sharing your ideas more often.

464
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:37,700
Yes, I know I can be a bit quiet in meetings.

465
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:41,300
Your ideas are valuable. Don't hesitate to share them.

466
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:45,200
I'll try to speak up more, even if it's just one comment.

467
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:47,400
That's a perfect place to start.

468
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:50,900
Sometimes I'm not sure when it's okay to interrupt.

469
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:54,200
You can say something like, can I add something here?

470
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:56,700
That sounds polite and natural.

471
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:59,900
It is. Most people will appreciate your input.

472
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:02,100
That makes me feel more comfortable.

473
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:05,000
Good. Comfort leads to confidence.

474
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,240
I also wanted to talk about my future in the company.

475
00:21:08,240 --> 00:21:10,400
I'm glad you're thinking about that.

476
00:21:10,400 --> 00:21:14,440
I'd like to grow and take on more responsibility over time.

477
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:16,000
That's a great goal.

478
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,080
What would you recommend I focus on next?

479
00:21:19,080 --> 00:21:23,520
Continue improving your communication and build leadership skills.

480
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:24,880
I'll work on that.

481
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:28,120
And don't be afraid to ask for help when you need it.

482
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:31,960
Sometimes I worry that asking for help looks weak.

483
00:21:31,960 --> 00:21:35,300
Actually, it shows awareness and professionalism.

484
00:21:35,300 --> 00:21:37,400
That's a new way to see it.

485
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:40,300
Strong employees ask questions.

486
00:21:40,300 --> 00:21:41,960
Thank you for saying that.

487
00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:44,800
You're doing very well, Anna. Keep going.

488
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:48,260
Thank you, James. I really appreciate your guidance.

489
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:51,100
Anytime. My door is always open.

490
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:52,900
Have a great rest of the day.

491
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:55,400
You too. Good luck with the project.

492
00:21:55,400 --> 00:21:59,060
That conversation felt very real and practical.

493
00:21:59,060 --> 00:22:04,660
Yes, and it shows how calm and clear communication builds trust at work.

494
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:09,660
Remember, you don't need advanced vocabulary to speak with your boss.

495
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:12,260
Simple sentences are powerful.

496
00:22:12,260 --> 00:22:18,060
Asking questions, giving updates, and sharing concerns are all professional skills.

497
00:22:18,060 --> 00:22:22,960
If you practice these conversations, you'll feel more confident over time.

